[figure] Rendered page 2 contains the Q1 graph as an embedded image. The text
  channel drops it entirely ("Create the following undirected graph" followed by
  nothing), so the figure file is the ONLY channel carrying it.
  Read it at native resolution, at 4x LANCZOS, and as 6x quadrant crops, then
  measured it programmatically:
    1. segmented the blue node discs -> 10 components, bbox centres recovered;
    2. for each node, scanned rings at r = 24, 32, 42, 55 px and grouped the
       angular positions of dark pixels into line-departure bearings;
    3. a bearing that is CONSTANT in r = a true incident edge; a bearing that
       DRIFTS with r = a straight line passing near, not through, that centre.
  This mattered: a naive centre-to-centre darkness test claimed edges (4,5),
  (4,8), (0,6), (0,9). The ring analysis shows all four are artifacts of
  near-collinear triples. The real edges are (5,8) [drawn straight through the
  disc of node 4], (0,4) and (0,1).
  Final reading, 15 edges:
    (0,1)(0,4)(0,5)(1,2)(1,6)(2,3)(2,7)(3,4)(3,8)(4,9)(5,7)(5,8)(6,8)(6,9)(7,9)
  Corroboration (2 independent checks):
    a) every node has degree exactly 3 -> 3-regular on 10 nodes, 15 edges. Almost
       certainly nx.random_regular_graph(3,10). A misread would essentially never
       land on perfect regularity.
    b) Q2's text says "remove node 1, edge (0,4) and edge (0,5)" -> the text
       independently confirms (0,4) and (0,5) are edges. They are.
  Recorded all of this in p1's brief, including the two traps, so the worker
  re-checks rather than rediscovers.

