[p3] spawn-worker p3 -> worker round 1, validator round 1 -> PASS. Done in 1 round.
  I had computed my OWN reference answers for Q5 in parallel (scratch/q5_check.py,
  py311 + networkx 3.3 + scipy) before p3 returned, specifically so I could check
  it rather than trust it. Line-by-line comparison:
    eigenvector, DEFAULT params, top10 values
      16485 0.491844 / 22777 0.419863 / 14618 0.177656 / 21928 0.139183 /
      2884 0.133813 / 1197 0.133186 / 9068 0.111138 / 17902 0.109219 /
      1324 0.100115 / 25747 0.094368                    -> exact match
    eigenvector, exact (_numpy) column                   -> exact match
    katz top10 order and values (16485 .2116, 22777 .1755, 9068 .1095, ...)
                                                         -> exact match
    average clustering 0.100539  (mine 0.1005391519)     -> match
    average Jaccard   0.041304  (mine 0.0413042246)      -> match
    lambda_max 8.762217, 1/lambda_max 0.114126           -> match
  My brief warned that eigenvector_centrality might not converge on a graph with
  4104 components. It DOES converge here; the worker checked rather than assuming,
  said so, and reported the default-parameter result as the headline answer -- 
  exactly right, since the question says "use default parameters".
  Better still, it independently noticed and explained the one thing I had spotted
  and not told it about: the default power iteration and the exact _numpy solver
  disagree on the ORDER of ranks 7 and 8 (9068 vs 17902). It traced this to
  NetworkX's stopping rule (L1 change < n*tol = 2.67e-2, a loose absolute bound on
  a unit vector), reported the residual, and showed that tightening tol reproduces
  the exact column. That is the correct diagnosis.
  Also correctly handled: alpha=0.1 admissibility vs 1/lambda_max=0.1141 (tight but
  valid); the raw-neighbour-set Jaccard convention, with the alternative
  convention's value (0.084305) given for contrast instead of being silently
  chosen; the degree<2 => c_u=0 clustering convention.
  Accepted as-is.
  NOTE FOR ASSEMBLY: p3 uses numbered \section/\subsection; its two tables carry
